@page "/health"
@attribute [Authorize]
@rendermode InteractiveServer
@implements IDisposable
@inject ChargeControlStatusHolder StatusHolder
@inject WebBuildInfo Build
@inject TimeProvider Time
@inject IServiceShutdown Shutdown
@inject IEnumerable<IVehicleUpdateService> VehicleFeeds

@*
    The whole of phase 0's user interface. It exists to prove the two things no unit test can: that a
    component in this assembly is reached by the host's dependency injection, and that it sees the
    same live objects the polling loop and the MQTT worker do.

    Everything on the page comes out of that container -- the build version, the app's configured
    time zone, and the status holder the poll loop writes to. Because the page subscribes to the
    holder's Updated event rather than reading it once, each completed poll pushes a new value down
    the circuit: the timestamp below advances on its own, and a page that sits still is telling you
    the poll loop has stopped while the web host is fine.
*@

<SiteTitle Page="Health" />

<h1>Health</h1>

<dl class="facts">
    <dt>Build</dt>
    <dd>@Build.Version</dd>

    <dt>Time zone</dt>
    <dd>@Time.LocalTimeZone.Id</dd>

    <dt>Last poll</dt>
    @if (_status is null)
    {
        <dd class="muted">no poll has completed yet</dd>
    }
    else
    {
        <dd>
            @TimeZoneInfo.ConvertTime(_status.Timestamp, Time.LocalTimeZone).ToString("yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss")
            <span class="muted">(@Age(_status.Timestamp) ago)</span>
        </dd>
    }

    <dt>Charge mode</dt>
    <dd>@(_status is null ? "—" : _status.Mode.ToString())</dd>

    @*
        The manufacturer feed (#140), on the page where "is anything wrong?" is actually asked. A feed
        blocked on its owner is the one thing here that no amount of waiting fixes, so it says which
        state it is in and quotes the service's own sentence rather than paraphrasing it.
    *@
    @if (_feed is not null)
    {
        <dt>Car feed</dt>
        <dd class="@(_feed.IsBlocked ? "error" : null)">
            @_feed.State — @_feed.Message
            @if (_feed.IsBlocked)
            {
                <a href="/vehicle-portal">Check the portal</a>
            }
        </dd>
    }

    @*
        How the feed's own running is going (#140), for the two questions a log grep was the only way
        to answer: is it actually reading on its clock, and is it holding one session?

        "Sessions" is the one to read first. One, however long the controller has been up, is the
        healthy answer -- more means portal sessions are expiring, which is the figure nobody had
        before this feed existed.
    *@
    @if (_diagnostics is { Attempts: > 0 } diagnostics)
    {
        <dt>Feed reads</dt>
        <dd>
            @diagnostics.Readings of @diagnostics.Attempts
            @if (diagnostics.LastReadingAt is { } lastRead)
            {
                <span class="muted">— last @Age(lastRead) ago</span>
            }
        </dd>

        <dt>Portal session</dt>
        <dd>
            @(diagnostics.SessionAge is { } age ? Duration(age) : "none")
            <span class="muted">
                (@diagnostics.Sessions sign-in@(diagnostics.Sessions == 1 ? "" : "s") since start@(diagnostics.Sessions > 1 ? " — sessions are expiring" : ""))
            </span>
        </dd>

        <dt>Next read</dt>
        <dd>
            @if (diagnostics.NextDueAt is { } due)
            {
                @TimeZoneInfo.ConvertTime(due, Time.LocalTimeZone).ToString("HH:mm:ss")
                <span class="muted">(@Until(due))</span>
            }
            else
            {
                <span class="error">never — the feed has stopped</span>
            }
        </dd>
    }
</dl>

@*
    The week's tally lives on its own page (#141): this one answers "is anything wrong right now",
    and that one answers "which of the two feeds should this site keep", which is a different
    question and a much slower one.
*@
@if (_diagnostics is { Attempts: > 0 } || _feed is not null)
{
    <p class="muted">
        <a href="/vehicle-feeds">What each feed has delivered</a> — cadence, agreement and coverage
        counted since the controller started.
    </p>
}

@*
    Stopping the service belongs on this page rather than the dashboard: it is not a charging
    decision, it is the process itself, alongside the build and the poll it reports on.

    Two clicks, not one. The stop is graceful and loses nothing -- that is the whole reason it exists
    -- but it is one-way from here: the page it is on goes down with the service, so starting it
    again means an ssh session on the Pi. A confirmation is the cheapest way to make sure the click
    was meant, and the text spells out what "stopped" costs.
*@
<section class="controls">
    <h2>Service</h2>

    <div class="control">
        @if (_stopping)
        {
            <p class="stopping">
                Stopping. The charger is being paused and the open session closed; this page will stop
                responding in a few seconds.
            </p>
            <span class="hint">
                Start it again on the Pi with <code>docker compose start gleanvolt-controller</code> in
                <code>/opt/gleanvolt</code>. Nothing restarts it by itself — that is what makes this a stop
                rather than a restart.
            </span>
        }
        else if (_confirming)
        {
            <label>Stop the service?</label>
            <button type="button" class="danger" @onclick="ConfirmStop">Yes, stop it</button>
            <button type="button" class="secondary" @onclick="() => _confirming = false">Cancel</button>
            <span class="hint">
                Charging stops, this UI and the Home Assistant integration go offline, and the
                controller stays down until it is started again from a shell on the Pi.
            </span>
        }
        else
        {
            <label>Stop the service</label>
            <button type="button" class="danger" @onclick="() => _confirming = true">Stop service</button>
            <span class="hint">
                The safe way to take the controller down: the charger is returned to its pause current,
                the open charging session is closed and written, and the session store is flushed —
                none of which happens if the process is killed instead. It does not come back on its
                own.
            </span>
        }
    </div>
</section>

@code {
    private ChargeControlStatus? _status;
    private bool _confirming;
    private bool _stopping;

    // The request returns immediately -- the host stops on its own thread -- so this render, and the
    // "stopping" message, actually reach the browser before the circuit goes away.
    private void ConfirmStop()
    {
        _confirming = false;
        _stopping = true;
        Shutdown.RequestStop("Web UI");
    }

    // The manufacturer feed's health, or null when no update service is configured (#140).
    private VehicleSourceHealth? _feed;

    // And how its running is going, when the service offers that -- a polling feed with a held session
    // has a session age and a next-due time; one whose API pushes has neither, which is why this is a
    // separate interface rather than more members on IVehicleUpdateService.
    private VehicleFeedDiagnostics? _diagnostics;

    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        // Seeded before subscribing, so a browser that arrives between two polls still sees the last
        // one rather than an empty page until the next.
        _status = StatusHolder.Current;
        RefreshFeed();
        StatusHolder.Updated += OnStatusUpdated;
    }

    // Raised on the polling loop's thread. InvokeAsync hops onto the renderer's synchronisation
    // context, which is what makes touching component state here safe.
    private void OnStatusUpdated(ChargeControlStatus status)
    {
        _status = status;

        // Re-read on the poll's cadence, like everything else on this page: Health is a cheap property
        // by contract, and a page left open is exactly where a feed's state changing has to show up.
        RefreshFeed();

        InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
    }

    private void RefreshFeed()
    {
        var service = VehicleFeeds.FirstOrDefault();

        _feed = service?.Health;
        _diagnostics = (service as IVehicleFeedDiagnostics)?.Diagnostics;
    }

    // The holder is a singleton and outlives every circuit, so an unsubscribed component would be
    // kept alive by its own event handler for as long as the process runs.
    public void Dispose() => StatusHolder.Updated -= OnStatusUpdated;

    // Clamped at zero: a clock stepped backwards by NTP must not read as a poll from the future.
    private string Age(DateTimeOffset timestamp)
    {
        var age = Time.GetUtcNow() - timestamp;
        if (age < TimeSpan.Zero)
        {
            age = TimeSpan.Zero;
        }

        return age.TotalSeconds < 90
            ? $"{age.TotalSeconds:F0} s"
            : $"{age.TotalMinutes:F0} min";
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// A span in the coarsest unit that still says something. A portal session is the thing this is
    /// for, and "1.4 h" answers "is it holding one?" better than 84 minutes does.
    /// </summary>
    private static string Duration(TimeSpan span)
    {
        if (span < TimeSpan.Zero)
        {
            span = TimeSpan.Zero;
        }

        return span.TotalMinutes < 90
            ? $"{span.TotalMinutes:F0} min"
            : span.TotalHours < 48
                ? $"{span.TotalHours:F1} h"
                : $"{span.TotalDays:F1} days";
    }

    /// <summary>How long until a moment, or "due" once it has passed and the fetch has not landed.</summary>
    private string Until(DateTimeOffset moment)
    {
        var remaining = moment - Time.GetUtcNow();

        return remaining <= TimeSpan.Zero ? "due" : $"in {Duration(remaining)}";
    }
}
